      'Russell, with Moore, Frege and later Wittgenstein, is counted among the founders of analytic philosophy, which treats philosophy as a matter of careful argument and logical analysis. His theory of descriptions was challenged, notably by P. F. Strawson in 1950, but it is still a starting point for the study of language and reference. The logical tools developed in Principia Mathematica shaped Kurt Gödel’s work on the limits of formal systems and, more broadly, the early history of mathematical logic and computer science.',
