      'Heidegger’s philosophy grew out of the crisis of Germany after the First World War: a country in political turmoil, and a European culture that seemed to have lost faith in its own certainties. In philosophy, he reacted against the idea that the human being is first of all a detached mind observing a world of objects. He took from Husserl the phenomenological method, which means describing experience as it actually shows itself, and he turned it towards ordinary, practical life rather than pure consciousness. Aristotle, Kierkegaard, Nietzsche and the Christian tradition also left deep marks on him.',
