      'Ludwig Wittgenstein was born in Vienna into one of the richest families in the Austro-Hungarian Empire. His father, Karl, was a steel magnate, and the family home was a centre of music and culture. Three of his brothers died by suicide, and the family’s tragedies and high expectations marked him throughout his life. He studied engineering in Berlin and then in Manchester, where he worked on aeronautics. His interest turned from engineering to the foundations of mathematics, and in 1911 he went to Cambridge to study with Bertrand Russell.',
      'He left Cambridge to work alone in Norway, then served in the Austro-Hungarian army throughout the First World War, and was taken prisoner in Italy near the end. During the war he finished the Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus, published in German in 1921 and in English in 1922. He believed that it had solved the main problems of philosophy, and he gave away his inheritance, which went to members of his family. From 1920 to 1926 he worked as a primary school teacher in small Austrian villages, among them Trattenbach, Puchberg and Otterthal. His teaching ended after an incident in 1926 in which a boy collapsed after being struck by him in class. There was a court inquiry, and he left the profession. He also worked briefly as a gardener and helped design a modernist house in Vienna for his sister Margarethe.',
