      'Albert Camus was born in 1913 in Mondovi, in French Algeria. His father, a farm worker, was killed in the First World War in 1914, when Camus was an infant, and he grew up with his deaf and nearly illiterate mother in a poor district of Algiers called Belcourt. A teacher, Louis Germain, noticed his talent and helped him win a scholarship, and Camus later dedicated his Nobel Prize lecture to him. He was a keen footballer and played in goal, until tuberculosis, which he caught at 17, ended that and kept him out of military service.',
