      'Camus belonged to the generation shaped by the Second World War, and his work grew from two experiences: the sunlit poverty of Algiers, and the moral emergency of the Occupation. The first gave him a deep sense of the physical world and a feeling for ordinary people. The second made him ask what, if anything, could justify resistance and what limits should hold even in a fight against evil.',
      'He was also a person of his time and place. As a French Algerian with a working-class background, he felt tied to both communities, and the war of Algerian independence, which began in 1954, put him in a very painful position. In January 1956 he went to Algiers to call for a truce that would protect civilians on both sides. He condemned both the torture used by French forces and the terrorism of the independence movement, and he hoped for a shared future. This brought criticism from many sides. His position, and his remark in 1957 about the risk of bombs on trams in Algiers where his mother might be riding, are still debated.',
